import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CDU0sSpB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects","description":"How Hearth makes sends, writes, deletes, publishes, and other real-world actions previewable, authorized, reconcilable, and recoverable.","frontmatter":{"title":"Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects","description":"How Hearth makes sends, writes, deletes, publishes, and other real-world actions previewable, authorized, reconcilable, and recoverable.","outline":false},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/design/effects.md","filePath":"en/design/effects.md","lastUpdated":1794111288000}'),s={name:"en/design/effects.md"};function i(o,e,d,c,l,p){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="approvals-takeover-and-external-effects" tabindex="-1">Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects <a class="header-anchor" href="#approvals-takeover-and-external-effects" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Approvals, Takeover, and External Effects&quot;">​</a></h1><p class="lead">The real risk is not that Hearth writes text. It is that Hearth sends email, changes files, submits forms, deletes content, pays, or publishes on the user&#39;s behalf. External actions need a clear preview, authorization, execution, and receipt; a model saying &quot;finished&quot; is not evidence that the result happened.</p><h2 id="design-goals" tabindex="-1">Design Goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-goals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design Goals&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Before an action happens, the user must see exactly what it will do: the target system, target object, key parameters, likely impact, reversibility, and required authority. Approval applies to that specific action, not a vague &quot;let the assistant use my mailbox&quot;. Parameters changing, scope expanding, or approval expiring requires confirmation again.</p><p>Every external action needs a verifiable result. Before execution the system records the pending intent; afterward it stores a receipt with time, target, result, and reversal method. If the network breaks or a service returns an uncertain result, Hearth reconciles before resending. A confirmed failure enters a queue that can be reviewed, repaired, and replayed.</p><p>Human takeover is part of the design, not a last resort. A user can pause a running task, keep current progress and completed actions, operate directly, and return the result for verification. Key actions and judgments during takeover are recorded so later improvements have evidence.</p><p>Approval is graded by risk and reversibility. Reading and organizing usually need limited authority. Sending, publishing, deleting, paying, and operating devices need stronger confirmation. Irreversible or high-value actions stay bounded by amount, target, or time even when pre-authorized.</p><h2 id="design-tradeoffs" tabindex="-1">Design Tradeoffs <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-tradeoffs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design Tradeoffs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>External actions follow a sequence: preview, authorize, execute, then reconcile. The system and its executors cannot bypass this path to call an external service directly. Connectors and desktop executors only complete actions inside their authorization; they do not decide whether the action should happen.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action type</th><th>Default policy</th><th>Exception</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Read, search, organize</td><td>Authorize within the task scope</td><td>Narrow scope and warn when sensitive data is involved</td></tr><tr><td>Create drafts or files</td><td>Execute directly, retaining artifacts and versions</td><td>Preview differences before overwriting an original</td></tr><tr><td>Send messages or submit forms</td><td>Confirm target, content, and scope each time</td><td>Low-risk repetitions may be pre-authorized inside a clear scope</td></tr><tr><td>Delete, publish, pay, or operate a device</td><td>Human confirmation by default</td><td>Never unconditional automatic execution</td></tr><tr><td>Actions that cannot be made reversible</td><td>Human takeover or explicit risk acceptance</td><td>Do not bypass through &quot;trying a few more times&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The authorization scope must be understandable to an ordinary user. An approval card explains &quot;who, what, when, and how far&quot; in natural language while preserving expandable technical details for verification. After a rejection, the system cannot rephrase and ask for the same action again.</p><p>Idempotency and reconciliation are the foundation of external actions. One send creates one message; one submission creates one record; retries reuse the same identity. When an external system returns no clear result, Hearth enters an uncertain state and queries or asks the user instead of executing again.</p><p>Approval binds the resolved real action, not the parameters first proposed by a model. Patches, full-permission operations, GitHub writes, and SMTP sends record an action digest and parameter fingerprint; changed parameters, expiry, or replay invalidate the approval. After a disconnect, timeout, or lost receipt, the action enters an explicit reconciliation queue where the user can confirm, retry, or abandon it instead of letting the system resend blindly.</p><h2 id="mail-sending-and-read-only-sync" tabindex="-1">Mail Sending and Read-Only Sync <a class="header-anchor" href="#mail-sending-and-read-only-sync" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mail Sending and Read-Only Sync&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mail sending reuses the same effect ledger instead of calling SMTP from the outbox page. When a draft selects real delivery with <code>smtp:&lt;accountId&gt;</code>, <code>OutboxService</code> creates an <code>email.smtp-send</code> approval. The preview shows sender, recipients, cc/bcc, subject, body, attachments, and the impact that &quot;sending cannot be undone&quot;. The TTL remains ten minutes, and the parameter hash binds recipients and content. After approval, the effect executor sends through SMTP and stores <code>receipt.json</code> (Message-ID, accepted/rejected, server response, and time). Success marks the draft <code>sent</code>; rejection or expiry produces no external effect.</p><p>Idempotency relies on the approval parameter hash, effect idempotency key, deterministic Message-ID, and a local receipt. A <code>sending.json</code> marker claims the effect directory first. If the directory exists without a receipt, the system treats the result as uncertain and never blindly resends. Authentication or envelope errors can be retried safely after clearing the marker. A network failure after sending, or a receipt write failure, is uncertain and asks the user to check the mailbox first.</p><p>IMAP reading uses a separate read-only path: <code>mailboxOpen(&quot;INBOX&quot;, { readOnly: true })</code>, the most recent 20–50 messages, and no read flag changes. Messages are deduplicated by Message-ID, written into the existing Source model, and their bodies go to the source content directory for later tasks or proactive use. Scheduled sync defaults to every 15 minutes and can be disabled per account. Real-provider TLS, app passwords, bounce handling, and spam semantics still need manual acceptance.</p><p>Human error is also input. A user may approve the wrong content, undo a correct action, or give conflicting instructions. The system keeps context and supports correction, compensation, and re-approval. The goal is a clear responsibility boundary, not pretending the system never makes mistakes.</p>',18)])])}const f=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
